<!--
  THE ONE HEADER EVERY COMPANION VIEW USES.

    Exocortex — <the app open on the glasses> — <connection>

  Chris, using the real build (2026-08-31), gave this a number: 80-85% of the
  companion screen should be the app's own content. What he was actually
  looking at was five stacked bands of chrome before a single line of session
  showed —

    1  faceclaw's ActionBar          "Faceclaw            Connected"
    2  the app-switch bar            "Ghost is open on the glasses   [Hub]"
    3  the companion's wordmark row  "GHOST      Rich view"
    4  the companion's status row    "20 in the feed - session ghost-..."
    5  the pane tab bar              [Terminal][Rich][Doc][Settings]

  Bands 2, 3 and 4 collapse into this single row, and band 1 is hidden while a
  companion is showing (see MainViewModel.companionActionBarHidden — the
  connection status Chris asked for in THIS row is the same status the
  ActionBar was already carrying, so keeping both would have added chrome
  while claiming to remove it). Band 5 stays; it is navigation, and it was
  shrunk rather than dropped.

  DELIBERATELY NOT GHOST-SPECIFIC. Nothing here names Ghost: the app segment
  is whatever the glasses report as foreground. This is the chrome half of the
  generalized per-app companion mechanism that is still queued as its own
  design job — that one decides WHICH view shows per app, this one is the
  frame all of those views sit in. A new per-app companion added later inherits
  this header by being dropped into main-page.xml's companion body; it does not
  have to build, or re-litigate, a header of its own.

  Binds to MainViewModel, inherited from the page — NOT to the per-app view
  model underneath it. Everything in this row is about the app/phone/glasses
  relationship, which no single app's companion owns.

  "Exocortex" is the tap target back to the hub, which is what replaced the
  app-switch bar's [Hub] button. The status segment keeps the ActionBar's own
  tap-for-the-full-string behaviour, so hiding the ActionBar loses nothing.
-->
<GridLayout xmlns="http://schemas.nativescript.org/tns.xsd"
  rows="auto" columns="auto,auto,*,auto,auto" class="exocortex-header">

  <Label col="0" text="Exocortex" tap="{{ onShowHubTap }}"
    class="exocortex-header-home" verticalAlignment="center" />

  <Label col="1" text="&#x2014;" class="exocortex-header-sep" verticalAlignment="center" />

  <!-- Whatever is foreground ON THE GLASSES. "Home" when that is the
       Exocortex home screen itself, which is what glassesForegroundLabel
       already reports for the cover glance. -->
  <Label col="2" text="{{ glassesForegroundLabel }}"
    class="exocortex-header-app" verticalAlignment="center" />

  <Label col="3" text="&#x2014;" class="exocortex-header-sep" verticalAlignment="center" />

  <Label col="4" text="{{ connectionStatusLabel }}" tap="{{ onConnectionStatusTap }}"
    class="exocortex-header-status" verticalAlignment="center" />

</GridLayout>
